REGIONAL REGULATION OF PROBOLINGGO REGENCY

NUMBER 2 OF 2019

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

THE REGENT OF PROBOLINGGO,

Considering:

a. that women and children are creatures of Almighty God who have human rights guaranteed by the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, therefore women and children must be protected from all forms of violence and discrimination;
b. that violence against women and children in the Region tends to increase, therefore the Regional Government and/or the community need to play an active role optimally to provide protection, so that women and children are prevented and free from violence or threats of violence, torture, or treatment that degrades human dignity and worth;
c. that protection of women and children is a mandatory concurrent affair which is the authority, obligation, and responsibility of the Regional Government, therefore regulation is needed as a basis for the implementation of comprehensive and integrated protection for women and children;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children.

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 12 of 1950 concerning the Establishment of Regency Regions within the Province of East Java (State Gazette of 1950 Number 41) as amended by Law Number 2 of 1965 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1965 Number 19, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 2730);
3. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 3143);
4. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 3668);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143) as last amended by Law Number 17 of 2016 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
7. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
8. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);
9. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Crime of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
10. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette 5234);
11. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Justice System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
12. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5512) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 24, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5657);
13. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation for the Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia number 64);
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 446);
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Empowerment of Child Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 42);
18. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903);
19. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 6 of 2015 concerning the System for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 903);
20. Regional Regulation of East Java Province Number 16 of 2012 concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Child Victims of Violence (Regional Gazette of East Java Province of 2013 Number 3 Series D, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of East Java Province Number 23);
21. Regional Regulation of East Java Province Number 2 of 2014 concerning the Implementation of Child Protection (Regional Gazette of East Java Province of 2014 Number 2 Series D, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of East Java Province Number 38);
22. Regional Regulation of Probolinggo Regency Number 6 of 2016 concerning the Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus.

With Mutual Approval

THE REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF PROBOLINGGO REGENCY

and

THE REGENT OF PROBOLINGGO.

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection of women and children is based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of children and women;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of women and children victims of violence; and
e. legal certainty.

Article 3

The implementation of protection of women and children aims to:
a. ensure the fulfillment of the rights of women and children so that they can participate optimally in society in accordance with human dignity;
b. provide protection and provide a sense of security to women and children from all forms of violence and discrimination;
c. prevent and eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children including trafficking in persons;
d. provide services to women and children victims of violence, reporters, and witnesses; and
e. empower women and children victims of violence.

Article 5

In implementing the protection of women and children, the Regional Government shall:
a. establish basic policies for the implementation of protection of women and children in the Regional Action Plan;
b. implement policies for the protection of women and children established by the government and provincial government;
c. coordinate the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children among regional apparatus organizations;
d. cooperate with other regions for the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children;
e. strengthen institutional Gender Mainstreaming and Children's Rights Mainstreaming for the implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the protection of the rights of women and children;
f. establish PPT in the Region;
g. ensure the implementation of excellent services in the protection of women and children, including for women and children victims of violence; and
h. prepare a data system for the protection of women and children, including data on violence against women and children;
i. establish and develop a Child-Friendly Regency;

Article 10

Violence against women and children may take the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect; and
e. exploitation.

Article 13

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 10 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner; and/or
d. forced sexual intercourse with another person for commercial purposes and or certain purposes.

Article 14

Neglect as referred to in Article 10 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the failure to fulfill the needs of the child properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social, committed by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or look after the child as appropriate, committed by parents, guardians or any other party responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting a person within the scope of their household, whereas according to the law applicable to them or due to an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/or
d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Article 16

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to receive:
a. protection;
b. information;
c. excellent service;
d. continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage;
e. confidential handling;
f. psychological and legal assistance; and
g. guarantee of rights related to status as a family member and community member.

Article 17

In addition to receiving rights as referred to in Article 16, child victims of violence also receive special rights in the form of:
a. the right to respect and full use for survival, growth and development;
b. the right to basic services;
c. the right to equal protection;
d. the right to be free from various stigma; and
e. the right to obtain freedom.

Article 22

1. The Regional Government conducts prevention as referred to in Article 21 letter a by:
a. socializing laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection of women and children;
b. providing counseling/guidance;
c. providing education about the dangers of domestic violence;
d. forming cooperation networks in efforts to prevent violence;
e. conducting coordination, integration, and synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
f. collecting data and information and forming a violence prevention system, mapping locations or areas prone to violence;
g. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of women and children;
h. increasing community awareness in behaving in accordance with social norms.
2. In addition to prevention efforts carried out by the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1), prevention efforts are also carried out by families and/or communities.
3. Prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is organized in an integrated manner by PPT

Article 23

1. Services as referred to in Article 21 letter b are organized through activities:
a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. social services;
f. legal aid and assistance services; and/or
g. repatriation and reintegration.
2. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) are organized with:
a. fast and precise;
b. safe and comfortable;
c. a sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easily accessible;
f. not charged; and
g. guaranteed confidentiality.
3. Services as referred to in paragraph (1) are organized in an integrated manner by PPT.

Article 25

1. The Regional Government is obliged and responsible to provide special protection for children.
2. Special protection for children as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be provided to:
a. children in emergency situations;
b. children in conflict with the law;
c. children from minority and isolated groups;
d. children who are economically and/or sexually exploited;
e. children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic substances, and other addictive substances abuse;
f. children who are victims of pornography;
g. children with HIV/AIDS;
h. children who are victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking;
i. children who are victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. children who are victims of sexual crimes;
k. children who are victims of terrorist networks;
l. children with disabilities;
m. children who are victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. children with deviant social behavior; and
o. children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
3. Provisions regarding the procedures for special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER VI

ESTABLISHMENT OF PPT

Article 30

1. In the context of providing protection for women and children, the Regional Government shall establish PPT as an integrated service center for women and children.
2. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) shall consist of elements of regional apparatus that administer affairs of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. youth;
d. social;
e. cooperatives;
f. industry;
g. manpower;
h. trade;
i. population and civil registration;
j. women's empowerment and child protection;
k. public peace and order;
l. regional general hospital;
m. police;
n. non-governmental organizations; and
o. universities.
3. PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Decree.
4.  Provisions regarding the organizational structure, duties and functions of PPT shall be further regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER VII

COOPERATION

Article 31

1. In providing protection for women and children, the Regional Government may cooperate with:
a. other regional governments;
b. commissions/agencies/government institutions in the field of protection of women and children;
c. police;
d. advocates;
e. health workers;
f. social workers;
g. volunteer assistants;
h. spiritual counselors;
i. non-governmental organizations engaged in the protection of women and children; and
j. the community.
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. socialization;
b. provision of legal aid;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. spiritual guidance;
h. repatriation and social reintegration;
i. Family Welfare Consultation Institution (LK3).
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented in accordance with laws and regulations.

CHAPTER VIII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 32

1. The community may participate in the provision of protection for women and children.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be in the form of:
a. establishing family partners at the sub-district/village level;
b. establishing non-governmental organizations whose field of work is in the protection of women and children;
c. conducting independent socialization of women's rights;
d. making prevention efforts if indications of violence against women and children are found in the community;
e. providing first aid to women and children who are victims of violence; and
f. reporting to the competent authority if violence against women and children occurs in their environment.
3. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be carried out by individuals, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, and the business sector.

Article 34

1. The Regional Government shall encourage the business sector to participate in the protection of women and children.
2. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of:
a. socialization to employees within the company regarding the rights of women and children;
b. socialization to employees within the company regarding the dangers of acts of violence against women and children;
c. providing assistance in the provision of protection for women and children as a form of corporate social responsibility.

CHAPTER XII

PROHIBITIONS

Article 41

Every person is prohibited from:
a. treating women and children in a discriminatory manner that results in women and children suffering losses, both material and moral, thereby hindering their social function;
b. treating women and children with disabilities in a discriminatory manner;
c. placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of children in situations of maltreatment and neglect;
d. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing violence against women and children;
e. committing violence or threats of violence to force women and children to have sexual intercourse with him or with another person;
f. committing violence or threats of violence, coercion, deception, a series of lies, or persuading women and children to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;
g. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing kidnapping, sale and/or trafficking of women and children;
h. obstructing women and children from enjoying their own culture, acknowledging and practicing their religious teachings and/or using their own language without neglecting access to community and cultural development;
i. placing, allowing, committing, ordering to commit or participating in committing economic and/or sexual exploitation of women and children;
j. intentionally placing, allowing, involving women and children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of narcotics and/or psychotropic substances and/ork. intentionally placing, allowing, involving, ordering the involvement of women and children in the abuse, as well as production and distribution of alcohol and other addictive substances.

CHAPTER XIV

INVESTIGATION PROVISIONS

Article 44

1. Civil Servant Investigators within the Regional Government are granted special authority to conduct investigations of criminal acts as referred to in the applicable Criminal Procedure Code.
2. The authority of investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) is:
a. to receive, search for, collect and examine information or reports concerning criminal acts;
b. to examine, search for and collect information or reports concerning criminal acts;
c. to request information and evidence from individuals or entities in connection with criminal acts;
d. to examine books, records and other documents concerning criminal acts;
e. to conduct searches to obtain evidence of bookkeeping, records and other documents and to seize such evidence;
f. to request the assistance of experts in carrying out the task of investigating criminal acts;
g. to order someone to stop, prohibit someone from leaving a room or place when an examination is in progress and to examine the identity of persons and or documents carried as referred to in letter e;
h. to photograph persons related to criminal acts;
i. to summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
j. to terminate investigations;
k. to take other actions necessary for the smooth investigation of criminal acts according to applicable law and which can be accounted for.
3. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) shall notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the public prosecutor through the Investigator of the State Police of the Republic of Indonesia in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the applicable Criminal Procedure Code.
